# Azure Secure Network Architecture Lab

## Overview

This project demonstrates the design and implementation of a secure Azure network architecture using Microsoft Azure networking services. The environment simulates a production deployment by separating workloads into dedicated virtual networks and subnets while implementing layered security through Network Security Groups (NSGs), User Defined Routes (UDRs), Virtual Network Peering, and a Network Virtual Appliance (NVA).
The objective of this project was to gain hands-on experience with Azure networking, traffic routing, and cloud security concepts commonly used in enterprise environments.

## Technologies Used

- Microsoft Azure
- Azure Virtual Networks (VNets)
- Azure Subnets
- Azure Virtual Machines
- Network Security Groups (NSGs)
- User Defined Routes (UDRs)
- Network Virtual Appliance (NVA)
- Virtual Network Peering
- Azure Portal

---

## Architecture

The lab environment consists of:

- **Production VNet**
  - Web Subnet
  - Application Subnet
  - Management Subnet

- **Security VNet**
  - NVA Subnet

- VNet Peering between the Production and Security VNets
- Custom routing to inspect traffic through the NVA
- NSGs securing communication between network segments

---

##  Features Implemented

- Created multiple Azure Virtual Networks and subnets
- Configured Network Security Groups with custom inbound and outbound rules
- Deployed an Azure Virtual Machine as a Network Virtual Appliance (NVA)
- Enabled IP forwarding on the NVA
- Created User Defined Routes (UDRs) to redirect application subnet traffic through the NVA
- Configured Virtual Network Peering between the Production and Security VNets
- Enabled forwarded traffic and gateway transit for VNet peering
- Validated secure communication between network resources

